 ],
 "literal": "Of the common ground kadamba tree, the awe-abounding deity / will punish cruel people, they say, at all. / A cruel person he is not, our hill-abounding land's lord. / Having loved, turned pale has the forehead. / To slip, wasted away have the large soft shoulders.",
 "translation": "They say the awe-inspiring deity dwelling in the kadamba tree of the common ground will surely punish those who are cruel. But the lord of our hilly land is not cruel at all. Yet, because of my love for him, my forehead has turned pale, and my large, soft shoulders have wasted away, making my bangles slip.",
 "modern": "மன்றத்தில் உள்ள கடம்ப மரத்தில் உறையும் அச்சம் தரும் தெய்வம் கொடியவர்களைத் தண்டிக்கும் என்று கூறுவார்கள். ஆனால், நம்முடைய மலைநாட்டின் தலைவரோ சிறிதும் கொடியவர் அல்லர். எனினும், அவர் மீது கொண்ட அன்பினால் என் நெற்றி பசலை நிறம் அடைந்தது; என் பெரிய மென்மையான தோள்களும் மெலிந்து வளையல்கள் கழலும்படி தளர்ந்துவிட்டன.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante in her distress. People in the village believe that the deity residing in the sacred kadamba tree in the common ground punishes those who are cruel or unfaithful. The heroine defends her lover, the lord of the hills, insisting that he is not a cruel man. However, her physical state reveals her inner suffering: her forehead has turned pale with pining, and her shoulders have grown so thin that her bangles are slipping off. This contrast between her words of defense and her physical deterioration highlights her deep love and the pain of separation in the Kuriñci landscape.",
